{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R12 — every system/hash a peer emits is one flat CBOR byte string: varint format code, then digest.",
    "",
    "Batch 3. The wire SHAPE of a hash, as distinct from its value (ECP-R2) and its receiver-side length",
    "check (ECP-R41)."
  ],
  "id": "ECP-R12",
  "id_status": "allocated",
  "title": "Every system/hash value a peer emits is a single untagged CBOR byte string whose leading LEB128 varint is a format code and whose remaining length is that code's digest length",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.2",
  "spec_also": [
    "§2.5 ('All hash values — `content_hash` fields, `included` map keys, and entity references in data — use the same flat byte representation')",
    "§7.3 (multicodec-style varint, normative)",
    "ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §5.3",
    "§9.1 (the floor row this id binds)"
  ],
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F20",
  "reading": [
    "§1.2: \"system/hash := bytes ; varint format-code + digest (variable) ; On wire: CBOR byte string",
    "(bstr)\" and \"A `system/hash` is a flat byte string. The leading bytes encode the format ... as a",
    "multicodec-style LEB128 varint; the remaining bytes are the raw digest.\" §2.5 extends the shape to",
    "every position: content_hash fields, included keys, and hash references in data.",
    "",
    "THREE WAYS TO FAIL IT, EACH SEEN IN THE WILD OF CBOR ENCODERS. (a) a text string — hex or the §1.2",
    "display form `\"ecfv1-sha256:\" + hex`, which §1.2 says is \"UI only, never on wire\"; (b) a structure —",
    "an array or map of {code, digest}; (c) a CBOR tag around the bstr — ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §6.3 forbids",
    "tags in data fields, and a tagged hash is not \"flat\". All three are asserted.",
    "",
    "THE LENGTH HALF IS ASSERTED FROM §1.2's TABLE, NOT FROM ECF §4.3. The two documents allocate different",
    "algorithms to codes 0x03 and 0x04 and disagree about whether the format code is an OCTET or a varint",
    "(F20). For the codes a posture-free run sees today (0x00, and 0x01 where negotiated) the two tables",
    "agree on length: 33 and 49 bytes. A hash whose code is 0x03 or 0x04 is witnessed, not scored, until F20",
    "is ruled — both tables give 32-byte digests for both codes, so length is unaffected, but which",
    "ALGORITHM is named is not, and ECP-R2's recomputation for those codes depends on it.",
    "",
    "WHICH POSITIONS ARE KNOWN TO BE HASHES. Only positions the suite can type without guessing: every",
    "entity's `content_hash`; every `included` key; and fields §3 types as `system/hash` in entities whose",
    "type the suite knows (signature target/signer; capability token grantee, parent, and granter when it is",
    "not a multi-granter). Bytes in an `primitive/any` data field are not assumed to be hashes."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "seed_policy",
      "content_hash_format"
    ]
  },
  "domain": {
    "axis": "hash_position",
    "members": [
      "entity content_hash",
      "included map key",
      "system/signature target",
      "system/signature signer",
      "system/capability/token grantee",
      "system/capability/token parent (when present)",
      "system/capability/token granter (single-sig form)"
    ],
    "note": "members not present in a run's observed traffic are reported not-observed, never passed."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "flat-bstr",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a completed handshake; every hash in a known hash position of every received frame",
      "why": "§1.2 / §2.5, once per domain member.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "connect",
          "capture": "handshake"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_hash_position_is_flat_bstr",
          "capture": "handshake",
          "why": "major type 2, no tag, decodable leading LEB128 varint, and total length = varint length + §1.2 digest length for 0x00/0x01. Other codes: witnessed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the assertion applied to fixture frames in which one content_hash is: the text `\"ecfv1-sha256:<hex>\"`; tag 37 wrapping the correct bstr; an array [0, digest]; the correct code with a 31-byte digest",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY, suite-side. The tagged fixture carries the correct bytes inside the tag, so an assertion that unwraps tags before checking passes it — that is the defect it exists to catch.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "replay_fixture",
          "fixture": "four frames, each with one hash position in a non-flat or mis-sized form",
          "capture": "fixture"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "each_hash_position_is_flat_bstr",
          "capture": "fixture",
          "expect": "fails"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a peer whose serializer emits hashes in their display form",
      "expect": "a conformant receiver's `included` lookup by bstr key finds nothing; a tagged hash is refused 400 non_canonical_ecf",
      "why": "§1.2's 'never on wire' is the rule this catches."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "encoding/hash_wire_format",
    "oracle_note": "provenance. It and `encoding/hash_algorithm_byte` cite ENTITY-CBOR-ENCODING §4.3 (the registry); wire encoding is §4.5, whose grammar reads `format-code = OCTET` against core's varint. Which reading those checks implement is a differential question (F20, F26)."
  }
}
